Rate Limiting means restricting how many requests a user/client can send to a system in a specific time period.
It is mainly used in APIs, servers, and microservices to prevent overload or abuse.

Simple Example

Suppose an API allows:

100 requests per minute per user

If a user sends:

80 requests → ✅ Allowed

100 requests → ✅ Allowed

101 requests → ❌ Blocked / rejected

The server may return HTTP 429 – Too Many Requests.

Why Rate Limiting is Important

Prevent server overload 🚫

Stop brute-force attacks

Avoid API abuse

Ensure fair usage among users

Control costs (cloud resources)

Example (Real Life Analogy)

Think of a water tap 🚰.

If water flows too fast → pipe may burst.

So we control the flow.

Rate limiting is the same idea for requests.

Example in API
Client → API Server

Request 1
Request 2
Request 3
Request 4
Request 5

If limit = 5 requests per second

5 requests → accepted

6th request → rejected

Common Rate Limiting Algorithms
1️⃣ Fixed Window

Example:

100 requests per minute

If user sends 100 requests in 30 seconds → next requests blocked until next minute.

2️⃣ Sliding Window

More accurate version of fixed window.

It calculates requests based on last time period, not a fixed minute.

3️⃣ Token Bucket

Requests consume tokens.

Example:

Bucket capacity = 10 tokens

Each request consumes 1 token

Tokens refill over time

If tokens = 0 → request rejected.

4️⃣ Leaky Bucket

Requests enter a queue and are processed at a constant rate.

If queue is full → requests are dropped.

Example in Spring Boot (Interview Level)

Using Resilience4j

@RateLimiter(name = "apiLimiter")
public String getData() {
    return "success";
}

Config:

resilience4j.ratelimiter.instances.apiLimiter.limitForPeriod=10
resilience4j.ratelimiter.instances.apiLimiter.limitRefreshPeriod=1s

Meaning:

Only 10 requests per second allowed.

